  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1039 PM CDT WED JUL 29 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening reveals a deep trough taking\nhold across the eastern US while ridging continues to dominate\nout west. A series of shortwaves will continue to rotate along\nthe periphery of the ridge, leading to low to moderate chances\nfor showers and storms Thursday and Friday. The greatest chances\ndaily will be to the south and west of a boundary lingering\nacross the state. Drier air moving into the region behind this\nboundary will keep the northern half of the area dry as we wrap\nup the work week. As we head into the weekend, an upper level\nshortwave will drop into the Tennessee Valley while pushing\nanother boundary our way. This would warrant medium to high\nchances for showers and storms through the weekend. Moisture\nlooks to pool ahead of this system with several members of the\nLREF suggesting PWATs well over 2\". Forecast soundings hint at a\nfully saturated profile with a warm cloud up to 15-16kft. This\nwould suggest periods of heavy rainfall are likely. There are\nsigns of this shortwave being cut off across the southeast and\nmeandering through early next week. This would keep elevated rain\nchances in our forecast. Details on rainfall amounts and \ndurations will be worked out in the coming days.\n\nThursday looks to be another fairly uncomfortable day with highs\nin the low to mid 90s. Continued muggy conditions will warrant a\nmoderate to major heat risk across our southwestern areas with\nheat indices around 105. Confidence is not quite as high in how\nlong we will see heat advisory criteria for as drier air\ncontinues to work in. Therefore, we will hold off on a Heat\nAdvisory for our southwestern areas for now. We will knock off a\ncouple of degrees daily as we head into the weekend as the ridge\nretreats back west. High temps look to settle into the 80s by\nSaturday.\n\n95/Castillo",
